<!--ID=5B95B1CC-2C7B-494F-B746-CF22A0E779B7;Version=1|{"Locales":{"en-US":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]},"en-GB":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]}}}-->
<speak xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis"
       xmlns:mstts="http://www.w3.org/2001/mstts"
       xmlns:emo="http://www.w3.org/2009/10/emotionml"
       version="1.0"
       xml:lang="en-GB">
  <voice name="en-GB-OllieMultilingualNeural">
    <prosody rate="-10.75%" pitch="-2.40%">
            Then a great windstorm arises, and the waves are breaking into the boat, so that the boat is already filling with water.<break time="500ms"/>
      Mark tells the scene with plain force: water entering, wind rising, the vessel taking on what it cannot safely hold.<break time="500ms"/>
      And in the stern Jesus is asleep on the cushion, not frantic, not pacing, not startled by what has startled everyone else.<break time="1000ms"/>
      His sleep does not mean He is absent from the boat; it means the storm has not undone His peace.<break time="500ms"/>
      The contrast is severe and memorable: the disciples are overwhelmed by what surrounds them, while Jesus remains at rest inside the same storm.<break time="500ms"/>
      Mark places the sleeping Christ in the middle of the violent scene so that the reader will see calm before calm is spoken.<break time="1800ms"/>
      The Lord is already present before the outcome is seen.<break time="1000ms"/>
      Jesus is present in the storm before the storm is quieted.<break time="1000ms"/>
      His rest is not fragile, and His presence is not cancelled by fear.<break time="1000ms"/>
      You are not alone when the scene around you feels full and loud.<break time="1800ms"/>
      You can rest.<break time="3800ms"/>
    </prosody>
  </voice>
</speak>